  <h2>What are the best overnight hikes in NSW for beginners?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Uloola Falls campground</strong> in Royal National Park is one of the
    better first walk-in overnighters we could confirm that you can reach by
    train. NPWS describes the Waterfall approach as an easy 2.5 hour walk on a
    fire trail. NPWS visitor information lists a non-flush toilet, no water and
    no mobile phone reception, so carry all your water, lodge a <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/safety/alpine-safety/kosciuszko-back-country-camping"
      rel="noopener">Trip Intention Form</a> and consider carrying a PLB.
    Campfires are not allowed, and the site is limited to one night. NPWS grades
    the full Uloola walking track Grade 3, so do not confuse
    the easy Waterfall approach with an easy whole-track rating.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Basin</strong> in Ku-ring-gai Chase National Park is a comfortable
    first night, with a 2.8km steep walk from West Head Road or a ferry from Palm
    Beach. NPWS lists toilets, drinking water, barbecues and cold showers only.
    Coverage is limited, but there is a public phone on site. If you drive, arrive
    before the West Head gates close: 8:30pm to 6am during daylight saving, or
    6pm to 6am otherwise. CampWatch notes that the lagoon has stingrays and that
    food should be sealed from bush turkeys and wallabies. It is a large,
    popular campground, so book ahead for weekends.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Little Beach campground</strong> in Bouddi National Park is a short
    gear-test night, not a hike. The walk from the Grahame Drive car park is 750m
    on a gravel management trail with short uphill sections. NPWS lists six tent
    sites, toilets and barbecues, but no water, so carry all your drinking water
    and camping water. Independent campground listings describe it as very hard
    to book, so treat it as a convenient test night rather than a reliable first
    overnight plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Great North Walk sections</strong> are a step up. NPWS grades
    Wondabyne to Patonga Grade 4 (23km, 8 to 9 hours) and describes it as for
    experienced bushwalkers; Time Out puts the first day to the Mount Wondabyne
    camp at about 11km. That camp is first come, first served (NPWS says it
    cannot be booked) and has no drinking water, so carry enough for the night.
    <strong>Berowra to Cowan</strong> is also a steep section: wildwalks grades it
    4 and reports about 13km via Berowra Waters, with about 5km of very steep
    track and 884m of climbing. Ridge Top campground has no water. NPWS lists a
    partial closure of the Berowra walking track (Apple Tree Bay to the Mount
    Kuring-gai access track) until 31 December 2026, so check it does not affect
    your section.
  </p>
  <p>
    On Great North Walk sections, water is the main thing to plan for. Several
    camps have none, so carry what you need and treat any creek or tank water you
    use.
  </p>

  <h2>Which NSW routes should you save for later?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Yuraygir Coastal Walk</strong> needs more planning than it looks.
    The Sandon and Wooli river crossings have to be timed with the tide or
    arranged by boat ahead of time, and some rock shelves are impassable at
    high tide or in big seas. Carry a tide chart. A short 1-2 night section is
    a way to try it, but NPWS grades the whole walk 4, so check the tide and
    river crossings for your section first.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Main Range</strong> in Kosciuszko is a Grade 4 loop (22km, 7 to 9 hours
    per NPWS), so we only suggest it as an overnighter in summer (roughly
    December to March). Nights can drop to near freezing even
    then, the weather turns quickly and the tops are exposed to wind. You
    can't camp in the lake catchments (Blue Lake, Club Lake and others) or
    close to water. Check your <a href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings/"
      >sleeping bag temperature rating</a
    > before you go, because a cheap summer bag is not enough here.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Green Gully Track</strong> is hut to hut, so you don't need a
    tent, but it is one of the hardest walks in the state: dozens of river
    crossings, wading that can reach chest height and sections with no clear
    path. The huts have mattresses, gas rings and rainwater tanks, but you still
    carry a sleeping bag, food and an emergency cooking stove, and you need to
    book. Leave it until you have several overnighters behind you.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Coast Track</strong> in Royal National Park is a great walk,
    but it is Grade 4 and 27km one-way. North Era, its old campground, has been
    closed since July 2023. NPWS now lists a platform camp at Garie Beach
    (room for two small tents or one two-person tent) that you must book in
    advance. Pre-booked campers can use untreated tap water at Garie Beach;
    otherwise take your last chance to fill up at the Beachcomber Avenue
    trackhead in Bundeena, and treat any water you find. Otford has a train
    station; from Bundeena you take the ferry to Cronulla for the train. An
    NPWS deer-control alert closes the Coast Track and Garie area from sunset to
    sunrise Monday to Thursday (excluding school holidays) from 6 July to 18
    December 2026; campers with bookings keep access.
  </p>

  <h2>What about overnight hikes in the Blue Mountains?</h2>
  <p>
    The Blue Mountains has its own dense network of overnight tracks and
    campsites within easy reach of Sydney, so it gets its own guide. If
    Katoomba, the Grose Valley or the Six Foot Track area is your target, start
    with our <a href="/guides/overnight-hikes-blue-mountains/"
      >overnight hikes Blue Mountains guide</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>How do you book campsites for overnight hikes in NSW?</h2>
  <p>
    NSW National Parks runs one online booking system for most developed
    campgrounds in the state, including many free ones (you usually pay a small
    booking fee instead of a camping fee). You choose the campground and date,
    and pay any camping fee when you book. Some bush sites are not bookable:
    Mount Wondabyne is first come, first served and Kosciuszko back-country
    camping needs no booking. Park entry fees are usually separate and charged
    per vehicle at parks with a gate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whatever the route, tell someone your plans: where you are walking, where
    you are camping and when you expect to be back. For remote trips you can
    also lodge a free <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/safety/alpine-safety/kosciuszko-back-country-camping"
      rel="noopener">Trip Intention Form</a
    >.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Even a free NSW campground is usually booked online, but a few bush sites
    are not. Check the park page for your site.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What fire rules apply to multi day hikes in NSW?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Cook on a gas canister stove.</li>
    <li>
      Check the <a
        href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/fdr-and-tobans"
        rel="noopener">NSW RFS</a
      > app or website for Total Fire Bans the day before you go.
    </li>
    <li>Don't light campfires.</li>
    <li>
      NPWS park fire bans (for example in Blue Mountains National Park and the
      Wollemi-Yengo reserves, 1 October 2026 to 31 March 2027) also ban
      solid-fuel stoves such as hexamine; gas stoves are allowed with
      precautions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What gear do you actually need for a multi day hike in NSW?</h2>
  <p>
    Route choice matters more than gear for a first overnight hike, but the
    gear still has to work. Keep the list simple: a pack sized to the trip
    length, a tent or hammock, a sleeping system matched to overnight
    temperatures, a gas stove, and enough water capacity for dry campsites.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Check your <a href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings/"
        >sleeping bag temperature rating</a
      > against the coldest night on your route, especially on the Main Range,
      and check owner reviews, since labels tend to be optimistic.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pair it with enough <a href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/"
        >sleeping mat R-value</a
      > to stop the ground from cancelling out your bag's warmth.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <a href="/guides/2-person-hiking-tent-guide/">2-person hiking tent</a> gives
      most solo hikers welcome extra room for gear on multi-night trips.
    </li>
    <li>
      Run through the full <a href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/"
        >overnight hike packing list</a
      > before a longer route like Yuraygir.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you are assembling a first kit from scratch, our <a href="/#kits"
      >beginner hiking kits</a
    > bundle a matched sleeping system and pack, and <a href="/build-my-kit/"
      >Build My Kit</a
    > fills in whatever you are missing without duplicating gear you already own.
  </p>
  <p>
    Looking further afield? Our <a href="/guides/multi-day-hikes-tasmania/"
      >Tasmania multi-day hikes guide</a
    > and <a href="/guides/best-multi-day-hikes-australia/"
      >best multi-day hikes in Australia</a
    > cover routes in other states.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    For your first overnight hike, choose a short walk-in like Uloola Falls
    campground or The Basin, carry all your water, and tell someone your plans.
    Once you have a trip or two behind you, try a Great North Walk section such as
    Wondabyne to Patonga (Grade 4), a short Yuraygir section or a summer night
    on the Main Range. Save the Green Gully Track until you are an experienced
    hiker.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>What are the best overnight hikes in NSW for beginners?</summary>
      <p>
        Short walk-in campgrounds are the easiest start. Uloola Falls, The Basin
        and Little Beach are options with different trade-offs: Uloola has no
        water or mobile reception, The Basin has a steep walk and limited
        coverage, and Little Beach is a 750m gear-test night that can be hard to
        book. Great North Walk sections such as Wondabyne to Patonga are Grade 4
        and a step up. Carry enough water for the night.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can you camp on the Coast Track in Royal National Park?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes, but only at Garie Beach, where NPWS lists a platform camp you must
        book in advance. North Era has been closed since July 2023. Check the <a
          href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/things-to-do/walking-tracks/the-coast-track"
          rel="noopener">park page</a
        > for updates.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Do you need to book campsites for multi day hikes in NSW?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Mostly, yes. Most developed campgrounds are booked online, including free
        ones, but some bush sites such as Mount Wondabyne are first come, first
        served. Whatever the route, tell someone your plans and when you expect
        to be back. For a remote trip, consider a trip intention and a PLB.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >What is the best multi day hike in NSW outside the Blue Mountains?</summary
      >
      <p>
        It depends on your experience. A short walk-in campground suits a first
        overnighter. Great North Walk sections, a short Yuraygir section or a
        summer Main Range trip suit hikers with a trip or two behind them, and
        the Green Gully Track is for experienced hikers only.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can you use a gas stove during a total fire ban in NSW?</summary>
      <p>
        Not reliably. NSW National Parks says visitor-owned gas cookers are
        generally prohibited on a Total Fire Ban day unless the park office
        agrees in advance, and campfires, liquid fuel (including metho) and
        solid fuel stoves are prohibited. Check the <a
          href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/safety/fire-safety/fire-bans"
          rel="noopener">NPWS fire bans page</a
        > and the park's alerts before relying on any stove on a Total Fire Ban
        day, and carry a no-cook meal.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How long is the Great North Walk?</summary>
      <p>
        About 260km one-way between Sydney and Newcastle. Most people walk it
        in short sections over several trips, and many of those sections make
        good beginner overnighters. See the <a
          href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/get-inspired/stories/why-this-260-km-hike-is-actually-not-that-hard"
          rel="noopener">park overview</a
        > for section options.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
